[FRA.FullName]
France 1940
[FRA.Description]

[FRA.Epilogue]
Despite the fact that the French fought well in many areas, the Germans destroyed the Allied troops in the field in a short time. The 51st Highland Division, which was not part of the group with the rest of the British army, was surrounded at Saint-Valery-en-Caux and was forced to surrender on June 12. On June 9, the Germans launched a major offensive on Paris, and on June 13, Paris was declared an open city as the French government fled to Bordeaux. The first German troops entered the French capital on June 14, just over a month after the start of the campaign.

The collapse of France was as sudden as it was unexpected. He upset the balance of power in Europe and overnight left the strategic assumptions on which Britain planned to fight Hitler completely obsolete. After France seceded from the Union in fact, the British war for the next four years was fought in the air, at sea and in the Mediterranean, but not on the Western Front. Only on D-Day, June 6, 1944, a large Allied army returned to France.

The population of France itself was demoralized, but not everywhere submissive. Resistance groups were created, but they risked bringing brutal reprisals against the civilian population in the event of an attack on the occupying forces.

[F001.FullName]
Lines of Defense
[F001.Description]
On May 14th, the Germans broke through near Sedan. Our troops are not able to resist the combined actions of tanks and dive-bombers and Belgian troops are retreating to defense positions in Antwerp. The British 1st and the 2nd Corps are still retaining positions from Wavre to Louvain where the 3rd Division under the command of General Montgomery is engaged in tough combat. The French 7th Army is reeling back on the seaward flank. In almost all the clashing points the power and rage of German offensive have been irresistible. In the Montherme area, Germans cut through the Maas by throwing ahead two Tank Divisions and capturing the fortified defense positions. For us it is vitally important to reestablish the defense line in this area.

[F002.FullName]
Confrontation
[F002.Description]
After a long period of the ‘Phony War’ when Britain and France had declared war on Germany following German invasion of Poland but neither side had yet committed to launching significant attack, on May 10, 1940 the German troops took the initiative and invaded the Netherlands, Belgium and Luxembourg. The French campaign was aimed at occupying these countries and forcing France to abandon the war. The German command planned to carry out combat operations in two stages (Operations ‘Fall Gelb’ and ‘Fall Rott’). Luxembourg capitulated without any resistance. The Netherlands and Belgium declared war on Germany. In the Netherlands the Army Group B under the overall command of General von Bock consisting of 26 Infantry and 3 Tank Divisions was engaged. The Allies sent their troops in to Belgium and Holland but on May 14 the Dutch Army surrendered. By May 21 the German troops approached the French cities of Amiens and Arras having knocked out most resistance by the Allies. The French Army needed to stop advance of the German troops by direct action.

[F003.FullName]
Covering Fire
[F003.Description]
As the result of the Nazi Panzer breakthrough to Abbeville on May 20, 1940, Allied Army Group 1 (10 British, 18 French and 12 Belgian divisions) became cut off and pressed to the sea in the vicinity of Gravelines, Arras and Bruges. From the west and the southwest they were in the advance line of the Army Group A forces under the command of General G. Rundstedt (tank groups of Generals E. von Kleist and H. Hoth, and 4th Army), from the east and the southeast the Army Group B under the command of General W. von Leeb (18th and 6th Armies) was advancing. The British Command had already made the decision to evacuate their troops on May 20.
The fact that on May 24 Hitler approved the order given on May 23 by Rundstedt to stop all tank groups at the Betun - Saint-Omer – Gravelines line, helped contribute to the success of the Allied evacuation. 
The advance of German infantry faced fierce resistance from the Allies. Then on May 27 the German command once again fed panzer groups into battle but the tanks could not break through British reinforced defenses. Finally, a big blunder by the German command led to successful evacuation of the major part of the Allies troops (more than 338,000 people).  Before Operation Dynamo started 59,300 thousand British were evacuated; from May 26 to June 4 another 278,800 people, including 139,800 British and 139,000 French and Belgians. Some 860 ships of the British and French Navies, fishing, transport and recreational vessels were involved in saving the Allied troops. About 224 of them were shipwrecked and about the same number were damaged. The British fighting aviation was covering the evacuation from the air. From May 27 to June 4, it performed 2,739 sorties and shot down 130 German planes losing some 302 friendly planes. The British lost about 68,000 men in addition to the majority of their combat vehicles and armament.

[F004.FullName]
The Heart of Resistance
[F004.Description]
The German advance in France developed quickly; the Allied troops were sustaining big losses and were continuously retreating. By June 4, UK and French troops cut-off from the main forces were evacuated to Britain. On June 10, the French government left Paris and on the same day Italy declared war on France. As battles waged throughout France, the Nazis were already approaching Paris. Northwest, in Normandy, the German troops were rapidly advancing towards the city of Caen, smashing all French pockets of resistance. The French command believed Caen could not surrender without fighting. A large group of French troops advanced to Caen and took up defensive positions around the city by June 12, 1940. 


[F005.FullName]
Under Siege
[F005.Description]
By the middle of June 1940, German troops occupied practically the whole of France. The British evacuated almost all of their troops leaving the French and the Belgians to their own fate. There were some minor areas though which continued to offer resistance to the invaders. In particular, the fortress port of Cherbourg proved a staunch holdout. Here the remnants of French troops were resisting fiercely but the final outcome was, by that time, clear to almost everyone.